# Summary
Introduced: the new merge-read authorization path permits loading bucket tags before requester authorization, and the HTTP TagResource path performs merge/validation using those tags before the final TagResource authorization.
TagResource now reads the existing bucket tagging document and merges the requested tags before the s3:TagResource bucket-policy/owner authorization check is performed. The new authorize_bucket_tag_control_merge_read helper only validates that the supplied account id matches the bucket owner; it does not verify that the requester is the bucket owner or is allowed by bucket policy. Because merge validation can fail before the later authorize_put_bucket_tag_control call, an unauthorized or anonymous caller who knows the bucket name and owner account id can distinguish cases such as whether the existing tag set plus a crafted 50-tag request exceeds the tag limit. This can leak limited bucket tag metadata, such as tag count/key membership properties. Before this commit, get_bucket_tags_for_tag_resource used the stricter owner-admin authorization path before loading tags.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [fb1678c](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/fb1678c) (`Authorize bucket tag merge reads before loading tags`).

The weak `authorize_bucket_tag_control_merge_read` path was removed. TagResource and UntagResource now pass the requested tag context and concrete policy action into `get_bucket_tags_for_tag_resource`, which runs the same bucket-policy/owner authorization used by the write path before loading existing bucket tags for merge/removal. The write path still re-authorizes before storing or deleting tags.

The fix also adds an AWS-facing regression test, `test_tag_resource_unauthorized_request_does_not_validate_merged_hidden_tags`, which creates a bucket with 49 hidden existing tags and verifies that an unauthorized alternate-account TagResource request adding two tags returns `AccessDenied` rather than leaking merged tag validation through `InvalidTag`.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the merge-read helper performs only bucket-owner account-id validation and not requester/bucket-policy authorization.
- [x] Confirm TagResource HTTP dispatch loads existing tags and merges request tags before final TagResource authorization.
- [x] Confirm merge validation can fail based on existing bucket tag state, specifically the 50-tag limit.
- [x] Demonstrate an unauthorized cross-account requester who knows bucket name and owner account id can reach the merge path.
- [x] Demonstrate observable differential results for two hidden tag states using the same unauthorized request.
## Report
Validated the finding as a real authorization-ordering information disclosure/oracle. Relevant code evidence: `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/bucket.rs:266-275` defines `authorize_bucket_tag_control_merge_read`, which only opens the bucket and calls `validate_tag_resource_account_id`; it does not check the requester or bucket policy. `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/bucket.rs:587-591` uses that weak authorization before loading the Tagging subresource. `crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:1172-1178` then parses existing tags and calls `existing_tags.merge(&tags)?` before `put_bucket_tags_for_tag_resource` is invoked at `mod.rs:1180-1185`. The real TagResource authorization is later in `crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/bucket.rs:311-335`, where policy/owner checks are performed. I added a targeted unit-test PoC that creates two private buckets owned by `111122223333`, one with 48 tags and one with 49 tags, then performs the same cross-account TagResource request as `444455556666` with two new tags and the correct `x-amz-account-id`. Command output: `48-existing-tags unauthorized TagResource result: AccessDenied`; `49-existing-tags unauthorized TagResource result: InvalidTag { reason: "tags cannot be greater than 50, got 51" }`; test passed. This proves an unauthorized requester can distinguish existing tag-count state because the merge/validation error is observable before final authorization. I also ran LLDB non-interactively. The debugger trace hit `authorize_bucket_tag_resource_action` only after `HttpFrontend::dispatch_routed` called `put_bucket_tags_for_tag_resource` at `bucket.rs:605` / `mod.rs:1180`, confirming the final auth path is deferred until after merge. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). A direct crash is not expected because this is a logic/authorization flaw, not memory corruption; the targeted debug test reproduced the security behavior without a process crash.

